  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(68.784pt, 499.03pt, " [36], enabling direct evaluation of stress components for ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 511.15pt, "cantilevers oriented along any in-plane direction in 128° Y-cut ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 523.15pt, "TFLN. ")
  #line-at(58.32pt, 535.27pt, "We note that the magnitude of σ₁ can vary with in-plane ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 547.39pt, "orientation in 128° Y-cut TFLN. This variation does not arise ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 559.39pt, "solely from elastic anisotropy, which primarily governs ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 571.51pt, "orientation-dependent stiffness, but instead reflects an ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 583.51pt, "underlying through-thickness residual-stress gradient. Such ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 595.66pt, "gradients can originate from multiple depth-dependent ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 607.78pt, "mechanisms, including processing-induced damage, interfacial ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 619.78pt, "effects, and non-uniform relaxation across the film thickness. ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 631.9pt, "Based on our measurements, however, we attribute the ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 644.02pt, "dominant contribution to anisotropic thermal expansion in LN, ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 656.02pt, "which induces direction-dependent thermal strain during ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 668.14pt, "cooldown and leads to asymmetric stress relaxation between ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 680.14pt, "the top and bottom portions of the film. Consequently, σ₁ ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 692.256pt, "reflects the through-thickness residual stress profile rather than ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 704.376pt, "purely elastic effects. ")
  #line-at(58.32pt, 716.376pt, "In isotropic thin films, both σ₀ and σ₁ can be reliably ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 728.496pt, "extracted from released beam profiles using curvature–rotation ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 448.99pt, "relationships [27]. However, this decomposition does not ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 460.99pt, "directly carry over to anisotropic TFLN. Unlike isotropic ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 473.11pt, "materials such as SiO")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.48pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(409.27pt, 474.07pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(412.51pt, 473.11pt, ", the total beam rotation in LN is ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 485.11pt, "strongly influenced by anisotropic bending stiffness and stress ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 497.23pt, "localization near the release junction, as shown in Fig. 8(a). As ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 509.35pt, "a result, the rotation can vary significantly with beam width ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 521.35pt, "and length, making σ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.48pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(415.39pt, 522.31pt, "0")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(418.51pt, 521.35pt, " highly geometry-dependent and ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 533.47pt, "difficult to extract reproducibly using curvature-based ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 545.47pt, "methods in 128° Y-cut TFLN. In contrast, σ₁ is determined ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 557.71pt, "primarily by the normalized curvature ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(483.1pt, 557.71pt, "ℎ/𝑅")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(502.18pt, 557.71pt, " and is largely ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 569.95pt, "insensitive to beam width and length, making it a more robust ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 581.95pt, "metric across different film thicknesses and device geometries. ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 594.1pt, "Finite element simulations further validate this approach by ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 606.1pt, "showing strong agreement between measured and predicted ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 618.22pt, "curvature behavior. Therefore, in this work, we focus on σ₁, ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 630.34pt, "the gradient stress component, as the primary measure of in-")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 642.34pt, "plane residual stress anisotropy in suspended 128° Y-cut ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 654.46pt, "TFLN. While direct measurement of through-thickness stress ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 666.58pt, "profiles may be pursued in future studies using techniques ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 678.58pt, "such as X-ray diffraction (XRD) [37], [38] or focused-ion-")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 690.7pt, "beam (FIB) strain-relief methods [39], [40], such approaches ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 702.696pt, "are beyond the scope of this work. Here, we instead adopt a ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 714.816pt, "device-based methodology that provides a complementary and ")
