  #line-at(54pt, 184.94pt, "To validate the extracted σ₁ values, FEA was performed ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 196.46pt, "using the stress model defined in (1)–(3). The measured σ₁ ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 207.98pt, "was directly applied as an initial stress condition in the TFLN ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 219.5pt, "layer, thereby enabling it to serve as a residual stress field ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 230.9pt, "after substrate release. In the simulations, one end of the ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 242.42pt, "cantilever was fixed to the bottom surface, while the ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 253.97pt, "remaining structure was left free to mimic a released boundary ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 265.49pt, "condition. The initial stress was assigned according to (1), ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 277.01pt, "where σ₁ denotes the through-thickness stress gradient ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 288.41pt, "responsible for beam curvature, and σ₀ denotes a uniform ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 299.93pt, "mean stress. This approach enables verification that the ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 311.45pt, "extracted σ₁ accurately reproduces the observed beam ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 322.97pt, "deformation and provides insight into the internal stress ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 334.49pt, "distribution via the in-plane stress component (Tₓ). ")
  #line-at(54pt, 346.01pt, "Fig. 8 (a) shows the simulated displacement and Tₓ for a 220 ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 357.41pt, "nm single-layer cantilever at θ = 0°. The simulation confirms ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 368.93pt, "the strong stress localization near the release junction, ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 380.45pt, "consistent with the interpretation discussed in the design ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 391.97pt, "section. This behavior explains why beam rotation is strongly ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 403.49pt, "influenced by local stress concentration and anisotropic ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 415.01pt, "stiffness, rendering σ₀ highly geometry-dependent and difficult ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 426.43pt, "to extract reliably using curvature-based methods. Simulations ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 437.95pt, "were performed from 0° to 175° in 5° increments for 100-, ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 449.47pt, "220-, and 460-nm films, and the resulting beam tip heights are ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 460.99pt, "shown in Fig. 8 (b). The simulated results (dotted lines) show ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 472.51pt, "excellent agreement with the measured data (solid lines), ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 484.03pt, "confirming that the extracted σ₁ accurately captures the ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 495.43pt, "deformation behavior. The small deviations are attributed to ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 506.95pt, "the omission of σ₀ in the simulation. ")
  #line-at(54pt, 518.47pt, "In the bilayer case, σ₁ was not measured directly for the ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 529.99pt, "individual layer thicknesses. Instead, it was estimated from the ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 541.51pt, "100 nm and 220 nm data using a thickness-dependent ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 552.91pt, "interpolation. This approach reflects the experimentally ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 564.43pt, "observed increase in σ₁ for thinner films (Fig. 5) by assigning ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 575.95pt, "them a higher weight. The interpolated σ₁ values were then ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 587.47pt, "used as input for each layer in the bilayer simulation. Fig. 8 (c) ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 599.02pt, "shows the simulated displacement and Tₓ for a bilayer ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 610.54pt, "cantilever (90/110 nm) at θ = 0°. Unlike the single-layer case, ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 621.94pt, "the bilayer structure exhibits opposite stress distributions in ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 633.46pt, "the ")
  #line-at(69.818pt, 633.46pt, "top ")
  #line-at(93.313pt, 633.46pt, "and ")
  #line-at(118.492pt, 633.46pt, "bottom ")
  #line-at(157.565pt, 633.46pt, "layers ")
  #line-at(192.096pt, 633.46pt, "due ")
  #line-at(217.265pt, 633.46pt, "to ")
  #line-at(235.841pt, 633.46pt, "their ")
  #line-at(264.844pt, 633.46pt, "reversed ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 644.98pt, "crystallographic orientations, resulting in cancellation of the ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 656.5pt, "gradient stress. Consequently, the net curvature is significantly ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 668.02pt, "reduced. However, complete cancellation is not achieved ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 679.54pt, "because the bottom layer is thicker than the top layer, leading ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 690.94pt, "to a residual compressive contribution that biases the ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 702.456pt, "deformation. The comparison between measured and ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 713.976pt, "simulated beam tip height profiles for the bilayer is shown in ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 725.496pt, "Fig. 8 (d). The simulation without σ₀ (red dotted line) captures ")
  #line-at(46.8pt, 737.016pt, "the overall trend but shows a noticeable offset from the ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 65.4pt, "measured data (solid line). Introducing an empirical mean ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 76.92pt, "stress offset of σ₀ = −2 GPa (black dotted line) significantly ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 88.46pt, "improved the agreement. We note that this value is larger than ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 99.98pt, "the reported mean stress of ~155 MPa in thick (~5 μm) LNOI ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 111.5pt, "films [22], but remains consistent with prior reports of GPa-")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 122.9pt, "level stress in submicron LN layers (e.g., ~1.05 GPa for 270 ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 134.42pt, "nm LN on sapphire) [21], indicating a strong thickness ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 145.94pt, "dependence of σ₀. In addition, the thermal expansion ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 157.46pt, "mismatch between LN and sapphire (α ≈ 14.4–15.9 × 10")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.48pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(550.06pt, 153.98pt, "-6")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(555.34pt, 157.46pt, "/K ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 168.98pt, "for LN [45] vs. 8.11 × 10")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.48pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(418.39pt, 165.5pt, "-6")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(423.79pt, 168.98pt, "/K for sapphire [46]) is larger than ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 180.5pt, "that between LN and Si (α ≈ 3.57 × 10")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.48pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(490.18pt, 177.02pt, "-6")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(495.58pt, 180.5pt, "/K [46]), further ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 191.9pt, "supporting the possibility of elevated mean stress in thin-film ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 203.42pt, "LN systems. While the magnitude of σ₀ is therefore physically ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 214.94pt, "reasonable, its exact value is obtained empirically to match the ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 226.46pt, "measured deformation and should be interpreted as an ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 237.98pt, "effective fitting parameter rather than a directly measured ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 249.5pt, "material property. The remaining mismatch in the 100°–175° ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 260.93pt, "range is attributed to local variations in layer thickness across ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 272.45pt, "the chip. ")
  #line-at(320.45pt, 283.97pt, "Overall, the strong agreement between measurement and ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 295.49pt, "simulation confirms the validity of the cantilever curvature ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 307.01pt, "method for extracting σ₁. These results suggest that the ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 318.41pt, "observed σ₁ originates from an orientation-dependent through-")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 329.93pt, "thickness residual gradient stress. A likely source is ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 341.45pt, "anisotropic thermal expansion of LN during cooldown, ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 352.97pt, "coupled with asymmetric stress relaxation between the film’s ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 364.49pt, "top and bottom portions. In addition, the observed stress ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 376.01pt, "reduction in the bilayer structure confirms that opposing ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 387.41pt, "crystallographic orientations can effectively mitigate gradient ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 398.93pt, "stress. ")
  #line-at(320.45pt, 410.45pt, "On the practical application of P3F LN in acoustic devices, it ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 421.99pt, "is important to note that the bilayer configuration in 128° Y-")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 433.51pt, "cut LN, despite consisting of oppositely oriented crystal ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 445.03pt, "layers, can preserve the effective piezoelectric response by ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 456.43pt, "maintaining constructive electromechanical interaction across ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 467.95pt, "the multilayer. In particular, the alternating crystallographic ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 479.47pt, "orientations reverse the sign of the piezoelectric coefficients, ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 490.99pt, "preventing cancellation of the induced electric field [41]. As a ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 502.51pt, "result, the P3F bilayer can achieve the same electromechanical ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 513.91pt, "coupling (")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(354.79pt, 513.91pt, "k")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(359.23pt, 513.91pt, "²) while also providing additional benefits, such as ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 525.43pt, "spurious-mode ")
  #line-at(384.882pt, 525.43pt, "suppression, ")
  #line-at(446.71pt, 525.43pt, "compared ")
  #line-at(498.253pt, 525.43pt, "to ")
  #line-at(518.153pt, 525.43pt, "single-layer ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 536.95pt, "platforms [43], [44]. At the same time, the bilayer ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 548.47pt, "configuration provides an additional mechanical advantage not ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 559.99pt, "available ")
  #line-at(362.548pt, 559.99pt, "in ")
  #line-at(383.414pt, 559.99pt, "conventional ")
  #line-at(448.154pt, 559.99pt, "single-layer ")
  #line-at(508.607pt, 559.99pt, "designs. ")
  #line-at(554.154pt, 559.99pt, "As ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 571.51pt, "demonstrated in this work, the opposing orientations generate ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 582.91pt, "gradient stress components of opposite sign, leading to partial ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 594.46pt, "cancellation of σ₁ and a significant reduction in structural ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 605.98pt, "deformation. This stress compensation is achieved without ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 617.5pt, "degrading piezoelectric performance, providing a pathway to ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 629.02pt, "simultaneously address both mechanical stability and acoustic ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 640.54pt, "performance in TFLN devices.  ")
  #line-at(403.63pt, 664.06pt, "VII.")
  #line-at(422.11pt, 664.06pt, "C")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(428.71pt, 664.06pt, "ONCLUSION")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(320.45pt, 680.26pt, "In this work, we investigated residual gradient stress in 128")
  #set text(font: "Batang", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(560.86pt, 680.26pt, "°")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(313.25pt, 692.496pt, "Y-cut TFLN using cantilever curvature. The results show a ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 704.016pt, "strong dependence of σ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.48pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(412.51pt, 704.976pt, "1")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(415.63pt, 704.016pt, " on crystallographic orientation and ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 715.536pt, "film thickness, with stress-free orientations and significantly ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 726.936pt, "larger gradient stress in thinner films. The extracted ")
  #line-at(313.25pt, 738.456pt, "normalized σ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.48pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(368.59pt, 739.416pt, "1")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(371.83pt, 738.456pt, " ranges from −0.1 to 3.4 MPa/nm (100 nm), ")
]

